A rejected submission provides only a binary signal; it does not identify which cells were wrong. Do not respond with a speculative near-variant based on the same demos. Revisit the complete model: output shape, transformation trigger, role/color mapping, changed-cell mask, and unchanged regions. If multiple materially different hypotheses remain and the demo budget permits, request a demo chosen to separate them. Submit again only when there is a specific evidence-based model revision that reproduces all demonstrations.

In this instance, two visibly different demo pairs showed localized changes: one recolored only selected 1-cells to 2 while other 8-cells remained, and another filled much of the region bounded by 8 while preserving boundary cells. A test guess that globally filled the region around the 3-cells was rejected. The demos did not validate that broad fill operation on the test; after rejection, the sound next move is targeted evidence gathering rather than arbitrary color or footprint changes.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/post-rejection-action-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-evidence-and-retry", "file": "references/identity-demo-interpretation.md", "content": "# Interpreting identity demonstrations

An input/output-identical demonstration is evidence, not a blank example. Record the complete identity result and ask what condition might distinguish it from transformed examples: object configuration, palette roles, dimensions, count, or spatial relation. Do not infer a universal motif-copying or expansion rule from one transformed example if another demo preserves its salient pattern. First test whether a coherent structural trigger explains both. If competing triggers predict different outputs for the test, request a further pair only when it can distinguish them; otherwise avoid a chain of requests that does not improve the test prediction. Replay every full pair under the proposed conditional rule before submitting.

In sparse-marker tasks, distinguish the marker field from a compact multicolor seed/object. A visible seed does not establish that every scattered marker instantiates that motif; test exact change masks and conditions before expansion.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-evidence-and-retry/references/identity-demo-interpretation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demonstrations: reconcile before transferring

When the same instance supplies demonstrations with widely different dimensions, background colors, seed colors, and motif shapes, do not assume the visible seed in the test is enough to derive a familiar operation. First record each pair's input/output changed-cell mask, foreground/background roles, and unchanged seed/background cells. Search for one role-level invariant that explains all pairs; explicitly reject candidate rules such as zero-component flood fill or global recoloring unless they reproduce every output exactly.

After each failed submission, revisit the transformation hypothesis rather than changing arbitrary thresholds or component definitions. If the demo cap is exhausted, use remaining submissions only for a rule supported by the full evidence. This instance's guesses of connected-background recoloring were not demonstrated by cross-pair replay and failed; the reusable lesson is to validate cross-demo invariance before committing, not to favor another particular rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-recovery", "file": "references/repeated-fallback-loop.md", "content": "# Repeated fallback submissions after rejection

An incorrect submission rejects the complete grid and gives no localization. Repeating the same blank/background-only candidate, or making cosmetic edits to it, cannot add information and wastes attempt budget. Before retrying, identify a materially changed hypothesis and check it against every complete demo, including output shape, role colors, changed-cell mask, and preservation. If no revised rule is supported, request a demonstration only when a concrete observation could distinguish live alternatives and change the test prediction. With heterogeneous demos, compare functional roles and changed masks; do not let one visually salient example override the rest.
